<div class="prose">
  <p>Unit 2 launches <strong>Thread 4: Science, policy, and the public</strong>. For the first time, students write for a non-student audience: a Greater Chennai Corporation planning meeting. The format choice (up to 600 words, a 3-minute annotated Google Earth video, or a one-page infographic) is deliberate UDL design: the rubric is identical across all three, so the deliverable assesses landscape-reading thinking, not writing stamina.</p>
  <p>It also continues <strong>Thread 1: OPVL and AI Documentation</strong> with new source types: government pages, satellite imagery, historical Google Earth imagery, and Chennai-local news.</p>
  <p>Four pages, nine locations, one new AI partner (the <a href="/ai-partners/field-geologist/">Field Geologist</a>). Each page works the same way: read it or watch it, then everyone does the same task. The summative, the <a href="/units/unit-2/assessment-landscape-reading/">Landscape Reading</a>, is a short-form biography of one location. Three checkpoints along the way give you feedback and do not go in the gradebook.</p>
</div>

